# A/B Test Plan
## Georgie's Pawsitive Pet Care

### 1. Page and Element Being Tested

The experiment will take place on the Pet Sitter
– Iowa City page of Georgie's Pawsitive Pet Care.

Version A will be the original page without a
personal photograph of Georgie.

Version B will contain one photograph of Georgie
added to the Meet Georgie section.

The photograph will be the only difference
between the two versions. All text, headings,
buttons, layout, and other page elements will
remain identical.

### 2. Hypothesis

Adding a personal photograph of Georgie will
increase the likelihood that potential customers
would contact the business.

Seeing the person who will care for their pets
may help visitors develop trust, feel more
comfortable, and create a personal connection
with the business.

### 3. Testing Method

AI visitors will represent Iowa City pet owners
looking for dependable in-home pet-sitting
services.

Each visitor will represent an Iowa City resident
who owns a dog, is planning a weekend trip, and
needs someone trustworthy to care for their pet.

Model and version: ChatGPT GPT-6

All trials will use the same model and settings.
Each trial will take place in a fresh conversation.

Visitors will be randomly assigned to Version A
or Version B before each trial. A randomized
assignment list containing 50 A assignments and
50 B assignments will be created before testing.

The same instructions and prompt will be used
for every trial, with only the website URL
changing.

Exact prompt:

"You are an Iowa City resident who owns a dog
and is planning a weekend trip.

You need to find a dependable in-home pet sitter
to care for your dog while you are away.

You are researching local pet-sitting businesses.
You want to feel comfortable with the person
entering your home and caring for your pet.

Visit and review this website:
https://gslange.github.io/pet-sitting/index.html 

After reviewing the website, would you feel
comfortable contacting this business to arrange
a meet-and-greet for your pet?

Answer YES or NO, followed by one sentence
explaining your decision."

### 4. Primary Outcome

The primary outcome will be whether the visitor
would contact the business to arrange a
meet-and-greet.

Responses will be recorded as YES or NO.
The percentage of YES responses will be
calculated for each version.

### 5. Sample Size

The experiment will include 100 total trials:
50 for Version A and 50 for Version B.

Data collection is expected to be completed
by November 24, 2026.

### 6. Comparison

The percentage of YES responses will be
calculated for each version.

The difference in contact interest will be
compared using a statistical test of proportions
to determine whether the difference could be
due to chance.

The hypothesis will be supported if Version B
produces a higher contact rate than Version A
with statistical evidence that the difference
is unlikely to be due to chance.
